# Unit 3 — What You Should Be Able to Explain

By the end of this unit, you should not just be able to define the vocabulary.

You should be able to answer questions such as:

### "Why would an attacker use ARP poisoning?"

Because manipulating ARP information can cause network traffic to be redirected through the attacker, potentially allowing the attacker to intercept or modify communication.

### "Why does network segmentation improve security?"

Because dividing a network into separate segments can restrict communication and limit how far an attacker can move after compromising one system.

### "Why isn't MAC filtering enough to secure a network?"

Because MAC addresses can potentially be spoofed, allowing an attacker to imitate an approved device.

### "Why would an organization use a DMZ?"

To place public-facing systems in a separate network segment, creating an additional layer of protection between those systems and the internal network.

### "What is the difference between NIDS and NIPS?"

A NIDS primarily detects suspicious activity and generates alerts, while a NIPS can detect suspicious activity and take action to prevent or block it.

### "Why can anomaly detection create false positives?"

Because legitimate behavior can sometimes be unusual. An activity that differs from the normal baseline is not automatically malicious.

### "Why does firewall rule order matter?"

Because firewall rules are evaluated in order, and the first matching rule determines the result.

